SEG = 'PER'
CAT = 'ftasPermits'
Q = [
    ("A Tasmanian licence holder plans to buy two rifles. How many permits to acquire are needed?",
     "Two", ["One", "Three", "None"],
     "Section 58(2) of the Firearms Act 1996 (Tas) requires a separate permit for each firearm to be acquired."),
    ("Your permit to acquire is issued on 1 March and you have not yet found the rifle. Under s 65, it lapses around:",
     "The end of May", ["The end of March", "The end of August", "Next March"],
     "Section 65 keeps a permit in force for 90 days from issue, or until the firearm is acquired if that comes first. Ninety days from 1 March runs to about 30 May."),
    ("A new licence holder who owns no registered firearm applies for a permit. It can be granted only after:",
     "28 days", ["7 days", "14 days", "90 days"],
     "Section 61(b)(i) sets a minimum of 28 days after lodging for an applicant who does not possess a registered firearm under a licence."),
    ("A licence holder who already owns a registered rifle applies for a second permit. Processing normally takes at least:",
     "14 days", ["24 hours", "28 days", "90 days"],
     "Section 61(b)(ii) says that in other cases the permit is granted after enough time to consider it properly, normally not less than 14 days."),
    ("Before a Tasmanian sport shooter can get a permit for a first pistol, they must have held the Category H licence and club membership for:",
     "6 months", ["28 days", "3 months", "12 months"],
     "Section 60(4)(c) requires a Category H licence for sport or target shooting and membership of an approved pistol club for at least 6 months."),
    ("After the waiting period, a new Tasmanian sport pistol shooter may be permitted up to how many pistols, each of a different type?",
     "2", ["1", "3", "4"],
     "Section 61(c) allows not more than 2 pistols of different kinds, such as one .22 pistol and one .177 air pistol, once the licence and club membership have been held for 6 months."),
    ("Your uncle gives you his rifle as a gift. Under Tasmanian law, you need:",
     "A permit to acquire", ["Only a signed receipt", "Nothing, as it is a gift", "A police letter"],
     "Firearms Services says a permit to acquire is needed to buy a firearm and also when someone is given one; s 10 requires both the right licence and a permit."),
    ("Do you need a permit to acquire to buy a spare barrel for your Category A rifle?",
     "No, if it matches your category", ["Yes, one permit per part", "Yes, unless a dealer fits it for you", "No, and any part is allowed"],
     "Firearms Services says a permit is not needed for firearm parts, but you may only acquire parts of the same category as your licence (s 107)."),
    ("Your permit to acquire has expired before you found the rifle. Firearms Services says you should:",
     "Destroy it and apply again", ["Ask for a 30-day extension", "Use it within 7 more days", "Hand it to the dealer to keep"],
     "Firearms Services says an expired permit should be destroyed and cannot be extended or re-issued."),
    ("Before issuing a permit to acquire, Tasmania Police may:",
     "Inspect your storage", ["Test-fire the firearm", "Interview your neighbours", "Ask for a range score"],
     "Applying for a permit may involve police inspecting the storage receptacle, and s 60(4)(d) requires the applicant to meet the storage and safety requirements."),
    ("On a permit application, Firearms Services asks you to explain:",
     "Why you need that firearm", ["Where you bought ammunition", "Your hunting results", "How much it costs"],
     "Section 60(4)(b) requires a sufficient reason and need to acquire the firearm, so the form asks why it is needed and what it does that your other firearms do not."),
    ("Under s 74, which act is an offence in Tasmania for an unregistered firearm?",
     "Possessing or using it", ["Photographing it for sale", "Reporting it to police", "Surrendering it to a dealer"],
     "Section 74 bans selling, acquiring, possessing or using a firearm that is not registered. Surrendering it to police is the lawful course."),
    ("A registrant sells a registered firearm through a dealer. Notice must go to the Commissioner within:",
     "7 days", ["24 hours", "14 days", "30 days"],
     "Section 80(1) requires the registrant to notify the Commissioner within 7 days after the sale; a dealer usually lodges the papers."),
    ("You discover your registered shotgun has been stolen. When must you notify the Commissioner?",
     "Within 7 days of finding out", ["At your next licence renewal", "Only if police ask about it", "Once your insurer has paid"],
     "Section 80(2) requires notice as soon as practicable but not later than 7 days after you become aware of the loss or theft."),
    ("The Commissioner may authorise someone who is not a dealer to sell ammunition only:",
     "In a remote area", ["At a shooting club", "During a hunting season", "To family members"],
     "Section 105(4) says the Commissioner must not authorise a person to sell or supply ammunition unless satisfied they intend to do so in a remote area."),
    ("Under s 100, an ordinary licence holder may send ammunition to a Tasmanian address by mail:",
     "Never", ["By registered post only", "With a permit", "In a locked box"],
     "Section 100 bans anyone from sending a firearm, firearm part or ammunition by mail to an address in Tasmania; only dealer-to-dealer sending is excepted."),
    ("You want to advertise your registered rifle for sale online. Under s 106 you may do so only:",
     "Through a licensed dealer", ["On a club notice board only", "With the permit number shown", "Once 28 days have passed"],
     "Section 106 allows an advertisement only by a licensed firearms dealer or for a sale arranged by or through one."),
    ("In Tasmania, private buyers and sellers must complete a firearm sale through:",
     "A firearms dealer", ["A police station", "Service Tasmania", "A club secretary"],
     "Tasmania Police's overview says firearms and firearm parts can only be bought and sold through firearms dealers."),
    ("Before selling you ammunition, the seller must:",
     "See your licence", ["Ring Firearms Services", "Check your safe", "Record your vehicle"],
     "Section 105(1)(b) requires the person selling or supplying ammunition to have seen the buyer's licence or written authority."),
    ("Tasmanian law limits how much ammunition you may acquire within which period?",
     "12 months", ["One month", "Six months", "Five years"],
     "Section 105(2)(b) caps acquisitions in a prescribed period, and regulation 22 of the Firearms Regulations 2026 sets that period at 12 months."),
    ("A courier business must not knowingly carry one package that contains:",
     "A firearm and its ammunition", ["Two unloaded rifles in cases", "Ammunition with no firearm", "A locked case with one firearm"],
     "Section 103 makes it an offence for a person in the business of transporting goods to knowingly carry a package containing both a firearm or firearm part and ammunition."),
    ("Under s 102, you may deliver a firearm to another person:",
     "In person or by a secure carrier", ["By ordinary post if insured", "By leaving it at their door", "Through any cheap courier service"],
     "Section 102 allows delivery only in person or through someone who appears able to keep the firearm, part or ammunition secure during delivery."),
    ("The Commissioner cancels the registration of a firearm in your name. You must:",
     "Surrender it to police at once", ["Sell it through a dealer within 90 days", "Re-register it yourself", "Store it until an appeal"],
     "Section 79(4) requires the registered person to surrender the firearm to a police officer immediately once its registration is cancelled."),
    ("You hold a firearm heirlooms licence and inherit your father's rifle. You still need:",
     "A permit to acquire", ["A dealer licence", "A minor's permit", "No other paperwork"],
     "Firearms Services says a person with an heirlooms licence who inherits the firearm still needs a permit to acquire it."),
]
